In this lesson we'll look at stem-and-leaf plots, a clever way to show every single data point while still revealing the shape of the distribution. Think of it as a sideways histogram that doesn't throw anything away. To build one, you split each number into a stem and a leaf. The stem is usually all the digits except the last one, so for two-digit numbers, the stem is the tens digit, and the leaf is the ones digit. Write the stems in a column in order, with no gaps, then pop each leaf next to its matching stem, and sort the leaves from smallest to largest. Always add a key, like "three pipe five means thirty-five," so a reader knows what the plot is showing. When you read the plot, check the shape by seeing where the leaves pile up, find the centre by locating the median stem, judge the spread by the smallest and largest stems that have leaves, and watch for any lonely stems with just one leaf sitting apart from the rest, because those are outliers. If you want to compare two groups, you can make a back-to-back plot with one group's leaves going left from the stems in reverse order and the other group's leaves going right. One quick warning: every leaf must be the same place value, so don't mix tens and ones on the same plot. Now let's see it in action.